batch-decode-eval-cache: evaluate the prompt cache state with every batched decode step.

GenerationBatch._step writes the caches but evaluates only the sampled tokens and logprobs, so the
cache writes build a lazy graph that keeps live Metal buffers until the whole batch drains. Metal's
resource limit counts buffers, not bytes: an engine whose batch never empties for about 10k decode
steps fails with "[metal::malloc] Resource limit (499000) exceeded" and its generation thread dies.
Prefill already evaluates the cache per chunk; this does the same per decode step.

Upstream: ml-explore/mlx-lm pull request 1911 (open), issues 1662 and 1672.
Drop this patch when the pinned release contains the fix.

--- a/mlx_lm/generate.py
+++ b/mlx_lm/generate.py
@@ -1428,10 +1428,18 @@
             )
 
         # Assign the next step to member variables and start computing it
-        # asynchronously
+        # asynchronously. The cache state is evaluated with it: the decode
+        # step writes the caches but nothing else materializes them, so the
+        # lazy cache-write graph would pin live Metal buffers each step until
+        # the batch drains, and hit the backend's resource limit.
         self._next_tokens = sampled
         self._next_logprobs = list(logprobs)
-        mx.async_eval(self._next_tokens, self._next_logprobs, token_context)
+        mx.async_eval(
+            self._next_tokens,
+            self._next_logprobs,
+            token_context,
+            [c.state for c in self.prompt_cache],
+        )
 
         # Eval the current tokens and current logprobs. After that also add
         # them to self.tokens so that it always represents the tokens contained
